his take on current affairs  a couple of weeks ago he received an  ominous email  an influencer agency based in london  contacted me which  is not unusual but what was strange was  in their very first email they said we  have some information here  that we would like you to disseminate  and we'll pay you for it  they asked him to discredit biontech and  reference supposedly secret information  that allegedly proves hundreds of people  have died from its vaccination  he played along and took up their offer  to find out more  i later got further instructions saying  for example that my posts  should come across as personal opinions  and that i should not mark them with  sponsored posts  which by the way is illegal in germany  and that i should say that i came across  this information myself  around the world youtubers are being  offered money in similar circumstances  like leo garcia who lives in france we  are at the center  of of something going on like  information war and people need to be  super super cautious of what they  read about what they hear about france's  health minister responded sharply to  these attempts to manipulate public  opinion  the vast majority of french people want  to be vaccinated  and i don't think anyone from france or  abroad can dissuade the french  with such a negative communication  campaign  according to research by german  broadcaster ard  youtubers from other countries have  already spread the dubious information  in india for example so here there are  two companies who provide vaccination  this brazilian youtuber is also seen  warning viewers against what he calls  the dangerous vaccine  there are lots of famous people out  there who will do almost anything for  cash  it's dangerous especially when it comes  to things like elections  influencers are being paid to place  certain messages  daniel laufer an editor at germany's  netspolitik.org news site has been  researching the issue  it's interesting that this company  doesn't even exist in the british  commercial register  it's a dummy company that doesn't even  have a doorbell at the address provided  and we found its customers seem by and  large mostly to be russian  miyako drochmann stopped researching the  topic  the supposed agency apparently figured  out he was leading them on  and cut all contact  [Music]  and we want to continue the discussion  with investigative journalist daniel  laffer who we just saw  in that report daniel welcome the  burning question raised in that report  is who is behind this campaign and so we  ask i guess who benefits  from the spread of this misinformation  it's not entirely clear who's behind it  but  where is this in general hard to say who  might  benefit from this information in this  case i think it's rather obvious  you have a case where someone is trying  to undermine trust  in a vaccine in which a lot of people in  the western world  are putting a lot of hope into they  basically are trying  to make it look as if the governments  were hiding something  the people behind this campaign wanted  the influences to claim that the media  was hiding it as well  so whoever is behind this wants  to create a situation in which it seems  like  um the western world is doing something  really  dangerous with their vaccine and of  course if you look at the situation in  russia with their campaign  in terms of their vaccine and not  developing the way they might have had  hoped it would  it seems possible that it's coming from  there we found traces  to russia in terms of the company that's  behind  this disinformation campaign and also if  you look at this twitter account for  sputnik v  the russian vaccine it has been  spreading similar messaging  to this disinformation campaign now for  weeks  daniel how much of an effect can  individual influencers have on  changing public opinion  you need to think about the way  influencers usually  work i mean they start talking  to their viewers directly they build up  an image where they become  something like perhaps a big brother or  big sister to their viewers so they have  a lot of trust and if  your big brother or your big sister  tells you something about their opinion  you of course  listen you take it very seriously this  is not like some  remote ad that's being shown to you this  is someone who you  really have a connection to or you think  you have a connection to telling you  something  which eventually might change your  political views which might uh  change the way that you feel about this  vaccine in this case  so i think if they actually succeeded  this campaign  which they did in some cases at least i  think it might have a great effect even  though it's hard to actually measure it  yeah we've been asking for a long time  now it seems what is being done  and what can be done to stop this  deliberate spread  of misinformation  the problem is it's very hard to  regulate it because  there's a thin line and if you cross it  you're basically  committing censorship and you do not  want to censor freedom of speech this is  not at all what you want to do obviously  so this  obviously means that you have to accept  a lot of things that are out there  that might be  wrong opinions in this case it's about  facts it's more easy  but the thing is also if you look at the  way they did it they took  real numbers and then put them into a  wrong context  so to figure out what was actually wrong  with those numbers you would have to  thoroughly fact  check this campaign this takes time  and eventually you need to draw this  conclusion  this is not something that you can  notice right away when just looking at  it  because it pops up in your feed so even  let's say if the platforms then reacted  to it and eventually took it down  and sanctioned the people spreading it  it would still mean that the message had  already gotten out  right and this makes it very hard to  actually uh  yeah somehow contaminate  contain this kind of kind of spread of  this information  daniel lover thanks so much  thank you

